Move the local repaint off bare Ctrl-L onto the escape key.

mosh triggers a full client-side repaint on a bare Ctrl-L (0x0C) and ALSO
forwards the byte to the server. That is hardcoded with no configuration knob.

For anyone using Ctrl-L as a tmux/nvim pane-navigation key (vim-tmux-navigator,
smart-splits.nvim and friends), every single pane switch therefore re-emits the
whole screen -- wasted bandwidth on exactly the flaky links mosh exists to
serve -- and there is no way to ask for a repaint without also sending a
keystroke to the remote application.

This drops the bare-Ctrl-L trigger and puts the repaint on the escape key
instead: `Ctrl-^ Ctrl-L`. The escape key is consumed locally and never reaches
the server, so the binding cannot collide with anything running remotely.

This is upstream PR #522 -- written by a mosh maintainer, open and unmerged
since 2014-05-18, resolving issue #370 (open since 2013-01-12):

  https://github.com/mobile-shell/mosh/pull/522
  https://github.com/mobile-shell/mosh/issues/370

Deliberately NOT taken from that PR: it also moves the prediction engine's
new_user_byte() calls out of the main loop into each branch. That is an
unrelated change, and a reviewer asking for it to be split out is part of why
the PR stalled. Escape-key bytes were already being fed to the prediction
engine before this patch, so omitting it changes nothing.

